## Part 2: Tier Protocols

### Tier 1 Protocol — Full Firewall

**When:** {{tier_examples.T1}}. *(Default example: money, inventory, tax, invoices, payments — anything where silent errors cost dollars or corrupt counts.)*

**Prerequisite:** Verification spec must be WRITTEN and verification tests must be WRITTEN (by a prior Verifier session) before build begins. If either is missing, Controller stops and flags in the flight plan.

**Step 0 — Manifest gate (before anything else fires).** Read
`manifests/<surface>/MANIFEST-INDEX.md` for every surface this increment touches. For every
touched layer, the required state is CERTIFIED (LIVE satisfies) (`manifest-format.md` §7;
SUPERSEDED never satisfies). Print the gate banner:

```
MANIFEST GATE — <surface> @ T1
  <layer>: <status> (<rows> rows) — PASS | FAIL (needs CERTIFIED (LIVE satisfies))
  ...one line per touched layer...
GATE: OPEN | CLOSED
```

This banner is the session's own gate declaration derived from the MANIFEST-INDEX; the
engine's packet assembler prints its own shorter machine banner (`BEHAVIORAL-MANIFEST GATE --
<surface> @ <tier> -- OPEN` + a REQUIRED READING line; on refusal, a reason line instead) —
both read the same INDEX, and the session banner is authoritative for the record.

If any touched layer fails, the gate is CLOSED: this increment does not fire, and **the next
dispatch is the extraction (or certification), not the build.** A design freeze without its
manifests is an incomplete freeze. The increment also declares its change move here —
**definition amendment** (manifest rows amended and certified FIRST; the build that follows
is conformance restoration against the amended rows), **projection change** (refactor-class:
full-tier conformance sweep before and after must show zero row deltas, or it was not a
refactor), or **conformance restoration** (defect-fix: cite the red row IDs this increment
turns green). An increment that declares no move, or fits none, is malformed — refuse it
(`manifest-format.md` §8; doctrine §9.1).

```
STEP 0 — PRE-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Dispatch Runner subagent.
  Runner runs: all tests in tests/verified/ (every prior increment).
  Results logged to: results/regression-run-pre-[date].json
  Gate: ALL tests must PASS.
  If any FAIL → Controller stops. Reports to flight plan. Does not proceed.

STEP 1 — BUILD
  Dispatch Builder subagent using the Builder Prompt Template (Part 4).
  Builder receives: build spec, codebase access, Principles 1-6 (instantiated).
  Builder does NOT receive: verification tests, verification spec.
  Builder implements, writes incremental artifacts, produces completion markers, commits.
  Builder reports back to Controller.

STEP 2 — VERIFICATION RUN
  Dispatch Runner subagent.
  Runner runs: tests/verified/[this-increment]/ against the build.
  Results logged to: results/verification-run-[date].json
  If PASS → proceed to Step 3.
  If FAIL → Controller stops. Writes failure details to flight plan.
    Operator diagnoses in next interactive session — recommended support: the
      operator-dispatched, read-only Diagnosis session (Part 5 recovery protocol,
      Part 4 template; different model family from the Builder).
    Operator may dispatch a new Builder with fix instructions.
    The original Builder is never shown test results directly.

STEP 3 — CODE QUALITY REVIEW
  Dispatch Code Quality Reviewer subagent using the Reviewer Prompt Template (Part 4).
  Reviewer receives: diff (base SHA to current), code-conventions.md, build spec.
  Standard review. If issues found:
    Dispatch a fresh Builder subagent to fix (not the original Builder).
    Re-dispatch Reviewer after fix.
    Loop until approved. Max 3 loops — after 3, stop the line.

STEP 4 — POST-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Dispatch Runner subagent.
  Runner runs: ALL tests in tests/verified/ (every increment, including this one).
  Results logged to: results/regression-run-post-[date].json
  Gate: ALL tests must PASS.
  If any prior increment's tests now FAIL → regression detected.
    Controller stops. Reports which tests regressed and which increment caused it.
    Does not attempt to fix.

STEP 4b — MUTATION PASS (OPTIONAL — grades the tests, not the build)
  After Step 4 passes, optionally dispatch a Runner subagent.
  Runner introduces representative mutants into a throwaway copy of the
    increment's changed code (diff-scoped) and re-runs
    tests/verified/[this-increment]/ against each mutant.
  Results logged to: results/mutation-run-[date].json (score + surviving mutants).
  Surviving mutant = operator FLAG (a weak test): a Verifier session strengthens it.
    Never the original Builder; tests/verified/ stays append-only.
  NOT a gate — never blocks the increment until a stable kill-rate baseline exists.
  Run manually per increment (the harness ships no scheduler).
  Doctrine + tooling: verification-architecture.md Part 8. T2: optional, same mechanics.

STEP 5 — FLIGHT PLAN UPDATE
  Controller writes to disk:
    - Layer 2: increment status → COMPLETE, verification status updated
    - Layer 2: any new Open Questions or Risks discovered during build
    - Layer 4: session log entry with Did/Decided/Verified/Flagged/Next
  Only after this write completes does Controller proceed to next increment.
```

### Tier 2 Protocol — Full Firewall (Cascade Variant)

**When:** {{tier_examples.T2}}. *(Default example: multi-step workflows where one action triggers downstream actions.)*

**Step 0 — Manifest gate (before anything else fires).** Read
`manifests/<surface>/MANIFEST-INDEX.md` for every surface this increment touches. For every
touched layer, the required state is CERTIFIED (LIVE satisfies) (`manifest-format.md` §7;
SUPERSEDED never satisfies). Print the gate banner:

```
MANIFEST GATE — <surface> @ T2
  <layer>: <status> (<rows> rows) — PASS | FAIL (needs CERTIFIED (LIVE satisfies))
  ...one line per touched layer...
GATE: OPEN | CLOSED
```

This banner is the session's own gate declaration derived from the MANIFEST-INDEX; the
engine's packet assembler prints its own shorter machine banner (`BEHAVIORAL-MANIFEST GATE --
<surface> @ <tier> -- OPEN` + a REQUIRED READING line; on refusal, a reason line instead) —
both read the same INDEX, and the session banner is authoritative for the record.

If any touched layer fails, the gate is CLOSED: this increment does not fire, and **the next
dispatch is the extraction (or certification), not the build.** A design freeze without its
manifests is an incomplete freeze. The increment also declares its change move here —
**definition amendment** (manifest rows amended and certified FIRST; the build that follows
is conformance restoration against the amended rows), **projection change** (refactor-class:
full-tier conformance sweep before and after must show zero row deltas, or it was not a
refactor), or **conformance restoration** (defect-fix: cite the red row IDs this increment
turns green). An increment that declares no move, or fits none, is malformed — refuse it
(`manifest-format.md` §8; doctrine §9.1).

**Identical to Tier 1** with one addition:

In Step 2, the Runner also executes **cascade completeness tests** — end-to-end tests that trigger step 1 of the cascade and verify that all downstream steps fire correctly. These are defined in the verification spec and written by the Verifier.

Individual steps may also have unit-level verification tests. The cascade test is what proves the chain works, not just the links.

Tier 1's verification-failure recovery applies unchanged at this tier — including the operator-dispatched, read-only **Diagnosis session** (Part 5 recovery protocol; Diagnosis Prompt Template in Part 4).

### Tier 3 Protocol — Spec Review + Code Quality

**When:** {{tier_examples.T3}}. *(Default example: data integrity, customer records, stateful operations — things that matter but aren't money.)*

**Prerequisite:** Build spec must exist. Verification spec is recommended but not required.

**Step 0 — Manifest gate (before anything else fires).** Read
`manifests/<surface>/MANIFEST-INDEX.md` for every surface this increment touches. For every
touched layer, the required state is EXTRACTED or better (`manifest-format.md` §7; SUPERSEDED
never satisfies). Print the gate banner:

```
MANIFEST GATE — <surface> @ T3
  <layer>: <status> (<rows> rows) — PASS | FAIL (needs EXTRACTED or better)
  ...one line per touched layer...
GATE: OPEN | CLOSED
```

This banner is the session's own gate declaration derived from the MANIFEST-INDEX; the
engine's packet assembler prints its own shorter machine banner (`BEHAVIORAL-MANIFEST GATE --
<surface> @ <tier> -- OPEN` + a REQUIRED READING line; on refusal, a reason line instead) —
both read the same INDEX, and the session banner is authoritative for the record.

If any touched layer fails, the gate is CLOSED: this increment does not fire, and **the next
dispatch is the extraction (or certification), not the build.** A design freeze without its
manifests is an incomplete freeze. The increment also declares its change move here —
**definition amendment** (manifest rows amended and certified FIRST; the build that follows
is conformance restoration against the amended rows), **projection change** (refactor-class:
full-tier conformance sweep before and after must show zero row deltas, or it was not a
refactor), or **conformance restoration** (defect-fix: cite the red row IDs this increment
turns green). An increment that declares no move, or fits none, is malformed — refuse it
(`manifest-format.md` §8; doctrine §9.1).

```
STEP 0 — PRE-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Same as Tier 1. Always runs. Every tier.

STEP 1 — BUILD
  Dispatch Builder subagent using the Builder Prompt Template (Part 4).
  Builder receives: build spec, codebase access, Principles 1-6 (instantiated).
  Builder writes own tests (acceptable at this tier).
  Builder commits, produces completion markers.

STEP 2 — SPEC REVIEW
  Dispatch Spec Reviewer subagent using the Spec Reviewer Prompt Template (Part 4).
  Reviewer receives: build spec (full text), implementer's report.
  Reviewer reads actual code — does NOT trust the report.
  If issues found:
    Same Builder subagent fixes (substrate sharing is acceptable at T3).
    Re-dispatch Spec Reviewer. Loop until approved. Max 3 loops.

STEP 3 — CODE QUALITY REVIEW
  Same as Tier 1 Step 3.

STEP 4 — POST-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Same as Tier 1.

STEP 5 — FLIGHT PLAN UPDATE
  Same as Tier 1.
```

### Tier 4 Protocol — Builder Self-Verified

**When:** {{tier_examples.T4}}. *(Default example: UI, notifications, display logic, low-risk features.)*

**Step 0 — Manifest gate (before anything else fires).** T4 is manifest-exempt: no
MANIFEST-INDEX read or gate banner is required before build dispatch. The exemption and its
reason are named in the receipt (`manifest-format.md` §7). The increment still declares its
change move here — **definition amendment**, **projection change**, or **conformance
restoration** (`manifest-format.md` §8; doctrine §9.1) — the three-move taxonomy carries no
tier exemption; an increment that declares no move, or fits none, is malformed — refuse it.

This banner is the session's own gate declaration derived from the MANIFEST-INDEX; the
engine's packet assembler prints its own shorter machine banner (`BEHAVIORAL-MANIFEST GATE --
<surface> @ <tier> -- OPEN` + a REQUIRED READING line; on refusal, a reason line instead) —
both read the same INDEX, and the session banner is authoritative for the record.

```
STEP 0 — PRE-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Same as Tier 1. Always runs. Every tier.

STEP 1 — BUILD + SELF-REVIEW
  Dispatch Builder subagent using the Builder Prompt Template (Part 4).
  Builder implements, writes tests, self-reviews per the template's checklist.
  Builder commits, produces completion markers.

STEP 2 — POST-BUILD REGRESSION GATE
  Same as Tier 1.

STEP 3 — FLIGHT PLAN UPDATE
  Same as Tier 1.
```

