## Part 7: Parallel Build, Serial Integration

Serial dispatch is the default. The Part 6 red flag — never dispatch multiple Builders in parallel — stands everywhere except under the regime this Part defines, and reverting to serial is always the safe state. This Part exists so that when a project deliberately enables parallel builds, the relaxation is governed, not improvised.

**1. The frame: parallel build, strictly serial integration.** Parallelism applies to the *build*, never to *integration*. The unit of parallelism is the **increment**; the serialization point is the existing **post-build regression gate**. Parallel Builders work in isolated git worktrees; their work re-enters the project one increment at a time through that gate. There is no new orchestration layer — the Controller already serializes integration; this regime only lets builds overlap.

**2. The independence predicate.** Two increments may build concurrently only if their **declared profiles** are pairwise independent on **all five** clauses: (1) disjoint file sets; (2) disjoint schema/state surface; (3) no shared invariant; (4) no runtime data dependency; (5) no shared mutable runtime resource (database, service account, external API key, fixture store — isolated or namespaced per worktree, per `core/methodology/least-privilege-isolation.md`). Independence is a **specification-level** property, not a merge-conflict property: textually clean merges routinely carry semantic conflicts — two increments can each pass alone and jointly break an invariant neither file-set reveals. The predicate therefore screens declared profiles at dispatch time; checking file names is not the predicate. T1/T2 increments and cross-cutting changes are excluded by definition and stay serial. Increments touching a declared high-blast-radius surface (the registry in `core/governance/AUTOMATION-ISOLATION.md`) are ineligible, full stop.

**3. The mandatory guardrails.** Independence is never assumed, only screened — so all four guardrails are mandatory wherever parallel dispatch is enabled. They are the regime, not tuning options:

- **Serial merge queue.** Parallel work integrates strictly one increment at a time, in dependency order.
- **Auto-rebase + retest before each merge.** Every increment is rebased onto the current integration head and its own tests re-run against that rebased state before it merges.
- **Full post-merge regression gate, per merge.** The full verified suite — never a delta scoped to the changed files. Cross-increment regressions are precisely what a delta scope cannot see; the full gate, not the predicate, is the true safety net.
- **Rollback-to-serial-by-default.** Any gate failure or rebase conflict halts the queue, reverts the integration branch to its last known-good state, and flips the project back to serial dispatch. Remaining queued work completes serially; parallel dispatch stays off until the operator re-decides it.

**4. Enablement: the precondition trio.** Before parallel dispatch is first enabled in a project, three declarations exist and are satisfied — a capability satisfying two of the three is not enabled:

- **Scope it** — the capability is declared in `core/governance/AUTOMATION-ISOLATION.md`: credential scope, environment and data posture, spend cap, soft per-run ceiling, and stop condition, per `core/methodology/least-privilege-isolation.md` and `core/methodology/spend-governance.md`. A worktree isolates the checkout, not credentials or data.
- **Stop it** — its halt action is registered in `core/governance/KILL-SWITCH.md`.
- **Revert it** — its rollback-to-serial path is documented **and tested** per `core/methodology/rollback-kill-switch.md`. Untested rollback is a hypothesis, not a rollback.

**5. The cap.** At most **3** concurrent worktrees. The cap is deliberately a fixed number in this document, not a configuration knob: raising it is a doc edit a project makes consciously, with the trade-off argued, never a parameter that drifts upward.

**6. Builder boot contract.** Each parallel Builder boots from a **self-contained dispatch packet**: declared inputs, declared target files, the increment's independence profile and the evidence for it, the tier, and this Part's rules. The contract names what a Builder receives, not the transport — today the packet is a kickoff prompt; any future packet assembler must satisfy the same contract.

**7. Crash-only Builders: artifacts, not transcripts.** A parallel Builder may be killed at any instant. All Builder state that matters lives as committed work in its worktree plus a **machine-readable result manifest** (a *result manifest* is a parallel-build recovery record — distinct from the *behavioral manifests* of `manifest-driven-builds.md` and from the memory engine's `dispatch-manifest.json` transport artifact; never shorten any of the three to a bare "manifest" where the referent could be ambiguous) — files touched, tests run and their results, self-reported tier, completion status. The merge queue judges committed artifacts and manifests only, never conversation transcripts. Worktrees are disposable: a killed Builder costs at most its current turn — discard the worktree and re-dispatch.

**8. Untrusted content.** A parallel Builder that consumes externally-sourced content falls under the untrusted-trigger rule and the credential/egress separation of `core/methodology/least-privilege-isolation.md`.

**9. Telemetry as record, dashboards as projection.** Every pilot or parallel run appends machine-readable telemetry records — append-only, one record per event. Any dashboard, summary, or cockpit view is **regenerated from the records, never hand-accreted**; a summary that cannot be re-derived from its records is not evidence. Builder/Verifier substrate identity — vendor and model id, recorded as separate fields — is taken from invocation/response metadata where available, never from hand-typed strings.

**10. Single-writer integration.** The serial merge queue is the **single writer to the integration branch**. Parallel Builders write only their own isolated worktrees; integration is the one serialized point where shared state is committed. The merge queue is the integration branch's single-writer lock — drafters draft, one writer commits — not merely a convenience ordering.

